even before announcing the inquiry the  government preemptively defended its  decision invoking the emergencies act  was a decision that was taken only after  careful consideration  the public inquiry will be given powers  to summon witnesses under oath and force  them to provide necessary documents it  will examine circumstances that led to  the convoy protests which gridlocked  downtown ottawa for weeks and choked off  traffic at canada u.s border crossings  and it will explore a key question was  the emergencies act a government  overreaction or necessary to restore  order in the nation's capital the act is  only to be used in the event of a  national emergency the act defines a  national emergency as an urgent  temporary and critical situation that  seriously endangers the health and  safety of canadians or seriously  threatens the government's ability to  preserve the sovereignty security and  territorial integrity of canada we are  talking about a very high threshold  whether it's you know espionage violent  extremism  or subversion or  you know other kind of very major  serious threats protest organizers say  the government overreached  at no time  did this convoy in ottawa  meet those standards it was a completely  inappropriate use the government insists  the danger and the damage went far  beyond ottawa the incredible  and devastating damage that was done to  public safety at ports of entry  in the form of interruption to  trade and travel the interruption to  supply chains the inquiry commissioner  is paul rulo he's spent 20 years as a  judge in ontario in a statement rulo  says i am committed to ensuring the  process is as open and transparent as  possible but it's unclear if the  government will release cabinet  documents related to its decision mr  speaker that inquiry will be useless  unless they waive cabinet confidence and  allow canadians to know the whole story  the government insists rulo will have  broad powers and access the ability to  compel witnesses and the compel the  production of documents subject as  always to lawful  privileges of evidence that may exist  bruno has until next february to report  his findings  ross lord global news
 

